  <h2>Designing for the Modern Athlete</h2>
  <p>In 2026, activewear is no longer just for the gym. The 'Lifestyle-Performance' hybrid is the dominant market force. Here is how to design products that meet both aesthetic and functional demands.</p>
  
  <h3>1. Strategic Seam Placement</h3>
  <p>Seams should follow the natural muscle lines of the body. This not only provides a 'sculpting' effect that customers love but also reduces stress on the fabric during high-intensity movement.</p>

  <h3>2. Ventilation Zones</h3>
  <p>Use laser-cut holes or integrated mesh panels in high-heat areas like the back, underarms, and behind the knees. This adds a technical look while significantly improving wearer comfort.</p>

  <h3>3. Minimalist Branding</h3>
  <p>The trend is moving toward 'Quiet Luxury' in activewear. Instead of giant logos, consider subtle tonal placements, reflective 3M details, or custom branded metal hardware for a more sophisticated feel.</p>

  <h3>4. Storage Solutions</h3>
  <p>Incorporate hidden pockets for phones, keys, and cards. For leggings, side drop-in pockets are the industry standard, while for bras, small internal pockets for keys are a highly valued feature.</p>

  <h3>From Design to Reality</h3>
  <p>At Fitclo, our design team can help you turn your inspiration moodboards into factory-ready technical designs. We focus on the details that make your brand unique while ensuring every piece is manufacturable at scale.</p>
